          <p>
            Prices move often. Tom’s Guide’s review recorded $348 on Amazon in mid-2025, and its later
            deal coverage has shown lower sale prices. WalkingPad’s store offers 15-day price
            protection: if the same version drops in price on its US store within 15 days of your
            purchase, it refunds the difference. Amazon has no equivalent promise, so if you buy there
            during a sale, that is the price you keep.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Warranty: one year</h3>
          <p>
            WalkingPad gives products bought on its site a one-year limited warranty, and the Amazon
            listing states a one-year warranty too. WalkingPad sells an optional extension through a
            third-party protection provider. One year is short for a machine whose motor and
            controller are the parts most likely to fail, and it is the same term as on the A1 Pro.
            Register the pad, keep the receipt, and before buying an extension, read who handles
            claims and whether it covers the motor. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            lists the questions to ask.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Returns: 30 days, with costs</h3>
          <p>
            Both routes give 30 days, on different terms. WalkingPad.com accepts returns within 30
            days of delivery, with prior authorization, in the original packaging where possible, and
            with all parts and any free gifts included. For change-of-mind returns the customer pays
            return shipping and original shipping is not refunded; if WalkingPad supplies a prepaid
            label, the cost comes off the refund, and its policy gives estimates starting at $100 or
            more for comparable pads such as the C2 and A1 Pro. Damaged, defective or wrong items are
            the company’s cost. Amazon’s listing gives a 30-day refund or replacement, notes that a
            return shipping fee may apply and that the item is not eligible for free returns. Either
            way, a change of mind on a 60 lb box can cost real money. Our guides to
            <a href="/treadmill-return-policies/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill return policies</a>
            and
            <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill on Amazon</a>
            explain how to avoid surprises. Keep the box until the window closes.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Recalls and safety warnings</h3>
          <p>
            On 20 October 2026 we searched the CPSC recall database for KingSmith and WalkingPad and
            found no recalls or product safety warnings for either name. That is worth checking for
            any walking pad: in April 2026 the CPSC told owners of Sperax walking pads and treadmills
            to stop using them after reports of uncontrolled speed, falls and fires, and the importer
            did not agree to a recall. If you buy a Z1, register it so the company can reach you, and
            search the brand on cpsc.gov again in the future.
          </p>
