              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">UREVO SpaceWalk E1L</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$169.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">35.4 x 15 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Differs by source</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">UREVO SpaceWalk 3S</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$249.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">42.5 x 16.5 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">265 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0–9% powered</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">UREVO Strol 2S Pro</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$549.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">42.5 x 16.5 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">265 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No; handlebar folds</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0–9% powered</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Z1 or A1 Pro?</h3>
          <p>
            The two pads have almost the same walking length — 47.6 inches against 47 — and both fold
            in half and arrive assembled. The A1 Pro costs $200 to $230 more on WalkingPad’s store and
            gives you three things for it: a 300 lb capacity instead of 242 lb, a slightly wider
            16.5-inch walking area, and FootSense automatic speed control alongside the remote and
            app. It is heavier, at 63 lb, and folds to a similar 32.5 by 21.5 by 5 inches. Both top out
            at 6 km/h — WalkingPad writes it as 3.75 mph for the A1 Pro and 4 mph for the Z1 — and the
            A1 Pro starts lower, at 0.5 mph. Both carry one year of warranty, and WalkingPad’s own pages put both motors in
            the “under 65 dB” band. If you weigh over about 210 lb, walk for many hours a day, or want
            wider margins, the A1 Pro is the better buy. If you are comfortably under the Z1’s limit
            and mainly want a long belt that stores away, the Z1 does the same job for less. Our
            <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking pads roundup</a>
            ranks the A1 Pro first for that reason and the Z1 as its value alternative.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Z1 or a UREVO pad?</h3>
          <p>
            The UREVO pads trade the Z1’s long, folding deck for a lower price or an incline. The
            <strong>SpaceWalk E1L</strong> costs about $130 less and is lighter, but its 35.4-inch belt
            suits slow desk walking rather than a brisk stride, and it does not fold. The
            <strong>SpaceWalk 3S</strong> costs about $50 less than the Z1 on UREVO’s store and adds a
            powered 0–9% incline and a 265 lb rating, with a 42.5-inch belt that is five inches shorter; it does not
            fold in half, so it needs a wider space to slide under. The <strong>Strol 2S Pro</strong>
            adds a fold-up handlebar and a running mode, at a much higher price on UREVO’s store. If
            you want to make walking harder as you get fitter, incline matters more than belt length;
            our
            <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads with incline roundup</a>
            compares the options. If you are over 242 lb, our
            <a href="/best-walking-pads-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads for heavy people</a>
            covers pads with real margin.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Z1 or a folding treadmill?</h3>
          <p>
            If you want to jog, run, or hold on, a pad is the wrong tool whatever its size. Our
            comparison of a
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-folding-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad and a folding treadmill</a>
            sets out what each gives up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-it-suits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Who the WalkingPad Z1 Suits</h2>
<p>
            <strong>It suits a desk walker in a small home who needs the pad out of sight.</strong> A
            long walking area that folds to 5.4 inches tall and rolls on wheels is exactly what a
            bedroom office or a shared living room needs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It suits taller walkers on a budget, if they are not heavy.</strong> A 47.6-inch
            walking area gives a natural stride at a brisk walk for most people; our
            <a href="/best-walking-pads-for-tall-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads for tall people</a>
            roundup explains the belt lengths that matter.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It does not suit anyone near 242 lb.</strong> Leave a margin of 10 to 15 percent
            below any rated capacity; for the Z1 that means walkers up to roughly 210 to 220 lb.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It does not suit joggers or anyone who wants incline.</strong> A 6 km/h ceiling,
            no handlebar and a flat deck make it a walking machine only.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It does not suit someone who carries it upstairs daily or wants long cover.</strong>
            Fifty-one pounds is a two-person carry on stairs, and the warranty is one year.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Setting up and looking after it</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Start with a mat on a hard floor.</strong> It protects the floor, cuts
              footfall noise and keeps carpet pile away from the belt. Our
              <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill mats roundup</a>
              covers sizes for pads.</li>
            <li><strong>Plug straight into a wall outlet.</strong> The cord is short, so plan the
              position around an outlet rather than an extension lead.</li>
            <li><strong>Lubricate and check tracking as the manual says.</strong> Our
              <a href="/how-to-lubricate-a-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad lubrication guide</a>
              shows the method on a low-profile pad.</li>
            <li><strong>Walk at your real desk speed for your real session length in the first
              weeks.</strong> Belt drift, noise and controller faults show up under sustained use, and
              the return window is 30 days.</li>
            <li><strong>Stop and contact KingSmith if the belt surges, will not stop, smells of burning
              or trips a breaker.</strong> Unplug it before any adjustment or cleaning.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Desk walking is gentle exercise, but if you have a heart, joint or balance condition, or
            are returning to activity after a long break, check with a clinician before adding hours
            of walking to your day. The CDC’s guidance of about 150 minutes of moderate activity a week
            for adults is a sensible target; our guide to
            <a href="/how-long-should-i-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long to walk on a treadmill</a>
            explains how to build up to it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the WalkingPad Z1 worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a walker comfortably under 242 lb who needs a long-belt pad to fold away under a bed or sofa, yes. At $299 it offers a 47.6-inch walking area and a 5.4-inch folded height, figures WalkingPad, Amazon and Tom's Guide agree on. Its motor and noise figures conflict between sources, the warranty is one year, and it has no incline or handlebar.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between the WalkingPad Z1 and A1 Pro?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both fold in half and have walking areas of about 47 inches. The A1 Pro costs $529 on WalkingPad's store against $299 to $329 for the Z1, and adds a 300 lb capacity instead of 242 lb, a slightly wider 16.5-inch walking area and FootSense automatic speed control. The A1 Pro weighs 63 lb against 51 lb. Both have a one-year warranty.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How small does the WalkingPad Z1 fold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                WalkingPad, Amazon and Tom's Guide all give a folded size of 31.8 by 22 by 5.4 inches, down from 56.1 by 22 by 4.1 inches unfolded. To store it under a bed or sofa, the gap needs to be a little more than 5.4 inches at its lowest point. Once folded it tips onto wheels and rolls.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the top speed of the WalkingPad Z1?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                WalkingPad lists 1 to 4 mph, or 6 km/h. Six km/h is 3.73 mph, which is the figure Tom's Guide gives, so the true ceiling is just under 4 mph. The remote changes speed in 0.5 km/h steps and the KS Fit app in 0.1 km/h steps. It is a walking pad, not a running machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the weight limit of the WalkingPad Z1?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                242 lb, or 110 kg, on WalkingPad's page, the Amazon listing and Tom's Guide's review. Leave a margin below any rated capacity; walkers above about 210 to 220 lb should look at a pad with a higher rating, such as the 300 lb WalkingPad A1 Pro.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the WalkingPad Z1 quiet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The claims conflict. WalkingPad's Z1 page says below 65 dB, Living.fit's listing says under 40 dB, and Tom's Guide measured an average of 57.3 dB with a phone app. The motor is brushless, which helps, but footfall carried through the floor is usually the louder problem, so use a dense mat and test it in your room during the 30-day return window.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Has the WalkingPad Z1 been recalled?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                We found no CPSC recalls or product safety warnings for KingSmith or WalkingPad when we searched on 20 October 2026. Register your pad with the company so it can contact you, and search the brand on cpsc.gov from time to time.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a WalkingPad Z1</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most disappointment with the Z1 comes from treating it as a smaller A1 Pro or a small treadmill. These are the four that matter.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying it close to the 242 lb limit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The Z1's capacity is 242 lb, and its motor rating cannot be confirmed. A pad run near its limit for hours a day works its motor and deck hardest. If you are above about 210 to 220 lb, pay the extra for the 300 lb A1 Pro or choose another pad with real margin.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Measuring the wrong gap under the bed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The Z1 folds to 5.4 inches tall, and the visible edge of a bed or sofa is rarely the lowest point underneath. Measure the lowest cross-rail or centre support, allow a little clearance for the wheels and your hands, and check that the folded 31.8-inch length fits between legs.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing on the motor or noise figure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Sources give the Z1's motor as 0.75, 2 or 2.25 HP, and its noise as under 40, 45 or 65 dB. Those numbers cannot be compared with each other or with other pads. Choose on belt length, capacity, folded size and return terms, and listen to it in your own room during the return window.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the cost of sending it back</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Both WalkingPad and Amazon give 30 days, but a change-of-mind return can mean paying to ship a 60 lb box, and WalkingPad's prepaid labels for comparable pads start at about $100. Measure your space and desk height before you order, keep the box, and test it properly in the first weeks.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

